+# Round 19 — Macindoe to Merle
+
+*A correction to the joint note's credit, ours to make; one ledger entry offered. Business
+paragraphs only; this round is a pull request, and the second key is the approving review, as
+agreed in R11 §13. It is based on `main` at `474125d` and touches no line that PR #8 touches. The
+personal half of the reply travels by mail.*
+
+---
+
+## 1. The note's credit for the counting dichotomy is wrong, and the sentence is ours.
+
+`NOTE-v1.md` says "The dichotomy is Macindoe's, published in [1]", and cites its upper half to
+Theorem 4.5 of [1] and its lower half to Theorem 4.6, with no prior literature. We held the pen
+on that page, and the claim came from [1] itself, whose abstract calls the dichotomy its "main
+new theorem". Two literature checks on our side (2026-10-18) found that the claim does not
+survive. Each source below was read at source except Brox, as marked. Our side found the error,
+not you, and it is ours to correct.
+
+**The upper half is classical.**
+- O. Rozier, *Problème de Syracuse : « majorations » élémentaires des cycles*, Singularité 2,
+  no. 5 (1991), 8–11. Théorème, p. 8: « Pour chaque entier n, il n'y a qu'un nombre fini de
+  cycles alternant n séquences d'entiers impairs avec n séquences d'entiers pairs ». Lemme 4,
+  p. 11, carries the same exponent `(α − 1)/(α^n − 1)`, `α = log₂3`, as [1]'s Theorem 4.5. With
+  his effective measure (p. 10, (5)) it bounds the number of odd steps for every number of
+  local minima.
+- J. Simons, B. de Weger, Acta Arith. 117 (2005), 51–70, made it explicit: Lemma 7 (p. 58),
+  `0 < Λ < m·c_m·2^{−((δ−1)/(δ^m−1))K}`; Lemma 14 (p. 61) and Theorem 3(d) (pp. 55–56), with
+  `K₁(m)/(m·δ^m)` decreasing to `15.107…`. That is [1]'s `n₀(p) = O(p·(log₂3)^p)`, from the same
+  Rhin measure.
+- T. Brox, Acta Arith. 92 (2000), 181–188: finiteness for each `m`. Not held on our side (the
+  publisher's server refused); characterized only as Simons–de Weger (p. 54) and Lagarias's
+  bibliography characterize it.
+
+Their `m`, the number of local minima of the Terras orbit, is [1]'s `p` exactly. Every block,
+including a block of entry depth 1, is one maximal odd run. Our own record had the
+identification wrong (`m = #{t : m_t ≥ 2}`); it is corrected and re-checked with fresh code
+(`cycles.md` §12.4, 47,498 checks, 0 failures). So [1]'s Theorem 4.5 is a rederivation of
+Rozier's and Simons–de Weger's trim by another route (rotation numerators and a max-plus
+recursion), in the same parameter. It is not a stronger statement and not a new one.
+
+**The lower half was anticipated in part.**
+- Rozier, p. 11, right after Lemme 4: « L'on voit ainsi que ce lemme est optimal si l'on part du
+  lemme 3 et si l'on ne prend pas en compte d'autres propriétés éventuelles des (m_i). » His
+  argument is a minimax over positive real run-length tuples of fixed sum. Its extremal tuple
+  grows geometrically at ratio `log₂3`, which is the staircase's profile.
+- Simons–de Weger, p. 69: "We believe that Lemma 7 is almost sharp for the worst case, in the
+  sense that at best we expect that the factor `m c_m` may be improved to a constant, but the
+  exponential dependence on `m` in the term `2^{−((δ−1)/(δ^m−1))K}` seems unavoidable." This is
+  stated as a belief; no configuration is given.
+- L. Halbeisen, N. Hungerbühler, *Optimal bounds for the length of rational Collatz cycles*, Acta
+  Arith. 78 (1997), 227–239, proved the analogue in the cycle-length parameter. Lemma 5 (p. 230)
+  gives the explicit word that attains their size functional at every length, which makes their
+  size criterion optimal (§4.4, p. 236). On p. 238 they draw the
+  moral: such an approach "*cannot* be successful", and further progress "would hence involve
+  number theoretical arguments", the rest reducing to the gcd condition over all rotations,
+  which is `q | R_r`. Their extremal words have about `0.59·n` odd runs, so they sit far inside
+  the trim and say nothing about the parameter `m`.
+
+What is [1]'s is the realization: integer configurations, in the block parameter, meeting every
+rotation's exact size condition at bounded slack `γ ∈ [3.683012, 5.140212]`, at every period
+(`cycles.md` §12.8.6). Our two checks did not find this in the literature. §2 adds its form at
+Simons–de Weger's own threshold.
+
+**The located obstruction.** The note's obstruction is the closing sentence of Theorem 4.6 of
+[1]. It stands as a statement, and the note keeps the quotation. Its moral is Halbeisen–Hungerbühler's
+in the length parameter, and Simons–de Weger expected it in `m`. [1] carries it into the block
+parameter. The note now says so.
+
+**The patch** (commit 1 of this PR, `NOTE-v1.md` only, `+13/−3`):
+- the credit sentence of "The counting dichotomy";
+- one attribution sentence added to each half;
+- one credit paragraph under the quoted obstruction;
+- four references, [5]–[8].
+
+Nothing in the note's argument changes. The note is co-authored, so this is a **proposed change
+for your key**, not a fait accompli. Strike or reword it freely, and the approving review is
+your key on it.
+
+We grepped `NOTE.md`, `LEDGER.md` and the breach map for the same credit and found nothing to
+change:
+- `NOTE.md` §1 already lists Halbeisen–Hungerbühler and Simons–de Weger in its provenance line.
+- L-A9's "Macindoe's round-11 proof of the all-`p` half" credits the integer construction, which
+  is ours.
+- The map does not state the dichotomy.
+
+## 2. An offer: L-A12, the sharpness at Simons–de Weger's own threshold.
+
+The refresh in §1 left one scope gap on our side. [1]'s sharpness is at threshold 1 (every
+`a_i ≥ 1`), while Simons–de Weger pose theirs for cycles whose minima are all `≥ X₀`. That gap is
+now closed in our record (`cycles.md` §12.8.7, 2026-10-19), and we offer it as **L-A12** at one
+key, ours, with yours invited. It is in the ledger's format, with the regime column filled. In
+short:
+- **Necessity.** Every configuration has `γ − 1 ≤ log₂(x_min + 1) < γ + log₂ p`. So the
+  threshold costs `log₂ X₀` and combines with the trim's `ε_p·n` as a maximum.
+- **Sufficiency.** For every `X₀ ≥ 1` and every `p ≥ p₁(X₀)`, the staircase construction run
+  under a weaker hypothesis meets every rotation's size condition with every element `≥ X₀`.
+  Under that hypothesis its last block continues the climb instead of collapsing to a unit
+  block. It does so at the scale where their Lemma 7, not their Corollary 5, is the operative
+  bound, with `Λ ≥ 0.054·2^{−ε_p·n}`.
+- **`p₁`.** It is explicit from the continued fraction of `log₂3`: 98 at their `X₀ = 5·2^60` and
+  110 at `2^71`. The first is consistent with their own size-only exclusion of `m ≤ 75` there.
+- **Unit-block staircase.** The staircase as first built, with a unit closing block, provably
+  cannot do this at that scale.
+
+What it settles of their p. 69 sentence:
+- the exponential dependence on `m` is attained;
+- `m c_m` cannot go below a constant (`0.054`).
+
+What it leaves open: whether `m c_m` can be lowered *to* a constant, their own "at best". On our
+side that is a `log₂ p` gap in the necessity, and nothing here decides it at the Lemma 7 scale.
+
+Grade: a size-only theorem. There is no divisibility test and no exclusion, and nothing is
+formalised. Against Rozier the entry is exact: his minimax value is realized in integers, but his
+Lemme 4 inequality itself is vacuous on the whole family, and the entry says so.
+
+Artifact: `experiments/staircase_x0_threshold.py`, 188 checks, 0 failures, re-run byte-identical
+modulo line endings.
+
+Why it belongs to the note, if you agree: it puts the staircase, the constructive face of the
+one wall, at the same threshold your L-A8 and L-A9 work at. Placement is yours to propose.
+
+## 3. Paper 1 v4: announced, not yet minted.
+
+The correction in §1 goes into [1] itself. A v4 is in preparation; it has no DOI yet. It will
+carry the same credit chain:
+- Theorem 4.5 attributed in its header to Rozier 1991 and Simons–de Weger 2005 (Brox 2000 for
+  finiteness for each `m`), with ours called a rederivation by another route, with `m = p`.
+- The abstract's "main new theorem" sentence dropped.
+- The sharpness stated with its proof and its credit: it realizes Rozier's 1991 extremal, which
+  Simons–de Weger expected in 2005, and the length-parameter analogue and the divisibility moral
+  are Halbeisen–Hungerbühler's 1997.
+- The threshold form of §2 included.
+
+The theorem numbers the note cites are kept. Until v4 is minted, [1] in the note stays v3 (DOI
+10.5281/zenodo.21730505). When it is minted, the reference update will come as a round of its
+own, with the version DOI.
